avif.decoder/--background--

 DESCRIPTION
   A Reggae decoder for AVIF (AV1 Image File Format) images. Input port 0
   takes the AV1 elementary stream in the format MMF_VIDEO_AVIF, which is
   what avif.demuxer produces, and output port 1 produces MMFC_VIDEO_ARGB32:
   one frame of four bytes per pixel, alpha first, top row first, with no
   padding between rows.

   The bitstream is decoded with dav1d, which is bundled into this library.
   MorphOS has no AV1 decoder of its own, so nothing has to be installed
   besides this library and avif.demuxer. The decoder is built as portable C
   for the 604e, without assembly and without threads, and decodes one stream
   at a time with film grain synthesis and all in loop filters enabled, which
   is what libavif and avifdec render.

   MMM_Setup on port 0 reads the geometry and the bitstream out of the struct
   AvifInfo which the demuxer publishes through MMA_ExtraData, and copies the
   streams so that the decoder does not depend on the demuxer's buffers
   staying alive. This is the only initialisation path, and it is done once:
   whichever of the setup on port 0 and the first pull on port 1 happens first
   does the work.

   The setup needs the demuxer to have been set up first, because until then
   there is no AvifInfo to read and MMA_ExtraData answers with nothing. A
   decoder which is set up before the demuxer is recoverable as long as the
   pipeline is complete before the first pixel is pulled: the setup fails, and
   the first pull on port 1 tries again and succeeds. The same goes for an
   input which is not an avif.demuxer: nothing publishes an AvifInfo, so there
   is nothing this class can do with the stream.

   The setup does not decode. Decoding happens on the first pull on port 1
   and is never repeated, so the whole picture is decoded and converted into
   one bitmap of width times height times four bytes before the first byte
   comes out. That first pull is where the decode time shows up.

   Colour conversion is done here, in scalar C. The samples are scaled to 16
   bit full range, converted with integer coefficients and reduced to the high
   byte, which keeps the 10 and 12 bit paths from throwing away precision
   before the matrix has been applied. The matrix is the one the container
   names in its nclx colour property when the file has one, and the one the
   sequence header names otherwise: the container wins, because it describes
   the colour the image was encoded in. BT.709, BT.2020, BT.601 and BT.470,
   FCC and the H.273 identity matrix are all handled, in full and in limited
   range, and BT.601 is the fallback for anything else. A monochrome image is
   converted from luma alone, and the chroma of a 4:2:0 or 4:2:2 image is
   upsampled bilinearly, since the chroma samples are co-sited with the top
   left luma sample of the block they cover and a plain shift leaves blocky
   edges.

   An image with a separately coded alpha plane is decoded by a second dav1d
   instance, and its single plane becomes the alpha byte. ARGB32 carries
   straight alpha, so the colour is left alone; an image whose colour planes
   were premultiplied is handed on as it is. An alpha stream which does not
   decode is not fatal: the image comes out opaque.

   The first picture of the stream is decoded and nothing else, so
   MMA_Video_FrameCount is always 1 and animation is not handled. The class
   implements no seeking.

 NEW ATTRIBUTES
   Attributes applicability:
     I - may be set at creation time.
     S - may be set on an existing object.
     G - may be get from an object.
     P - may be set for an object's port.
     Q - may be queried from an object's port.

   MMA_DataFormat         (V1)    [..G.Q], STRPTR
   MMA_MediaType          (V1)    [..G.Q], ULONG
   MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel (V1)    [..G.Q], ULONG
   MMA_Video_FrameCount   (V1)    [..G.Q], LONG
   MMA_Video_Height       (V1)    [..G.Q], LONG
   MMA_Video_UseAlpha     (V1)    [..G.Q], BOOL
   MMA_Video_Width        (V1)    [..G.Q], LONG

   All of them answer what the input published when this class took its data
   in. None of them may be set.

 NEW METHODS
   MMM_Pull(port, buffer, length)  (V1)

 HISTORY
   1.1 (15.09.2026)
   - Initial release.

avif.decoder/MMA_DataFormat

 NAME
   MMA_DataFormat (V1) [..G.Q], STRPTR

 FUNCTION
   Returns "AVIF" as a string constant. It names the format the image came
   in, not the format of the output, and does not change.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.decoder/MMA_MediaType

avif.decoder/MMA_MediaType

 NAME
   MMA_MediaType (V1) [..G.Q], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Returns MMT_PICTURE. The decoder produces one still image and never more.

avif.decoder/MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel

 NAME
   MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel (V1) [..G.Q], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Luma bit depth the image was coded with: 8, 10 or 12, as published by the
   demuxer, and 0 until this class has taken its data in.

 NOTES
   This is the depth of the coded image, not of the output. Port 1 is
   MMFC_VIDEO_ARGB32, which is eight bits per channel whatever this says: a
   10 or 12 bit image is reduced to 8 bits per channel here, in the colour
   conversion, by scaling the samples onto the full range rather than by
   dropping their low bytes.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.decoder/MMA_Video_Width, avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel

avif.decoder/MMA_Video_FrameCount

 NAME
   MMA_Video_FrameCount (V1) [..G.Q], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Returns 1, and 1 also before the data has been taken in. Only the first
   picture of the stream is decoded, so there is nothing to count beyond it.

 NOTES
   On MorphOS the multimedia framework stores this attribute in a LONG, and
   this class writes it as one. A QUAD variable on the receiving side is fine,
   but do not let the framework write a UQUAD into it: use the 32 bit form of
   the attribute macro.

avif.decoder/MMA_Video_Height

 NAME
   MMA_Video_Height (V1) [..G.Q], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Height of the decoded picture in pixels, as published by the demuxer, and 0
   until this class has taken its data in. A geometry of 0 or of 65536 and
   more is refused, since neither can be a picture this class can decode.

   The decoded picture has to come out at the published height. A picture
   which comes out at another size fails the conversion, and the pull which
   triggers the decode returns 0.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.decoder/MMA_Video_Width, avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_Height

avif.decoder/MMA_Video_UseAlpha

 NAME
   MMA_Video_UseAlpha (V1) [..G.Q], BOOL

 FUNCTION
   TRUE when the image codes its alpha channel as an item of its own, as
   published by the demuxer, and FALSE otherwise and before this class has
   taken its data in.

 NOTES
   The pixels on port 1 always have an alpha byte, whether this says TRUE or
   not: without an alpha item it is 255 in every pixel.

   An image whose alpha was premultiplied into the colour planes has no
   separate alpha item and reports FALSE. Nothing is done to the colour in
   that case either: ARGB32 carries straight alpha, and a premultiplied image
   is handed on with its colour already scaled.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.demuxer/MMA_ExtraData, avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_UseAlpha

avif.decoder/MMA_Video_Width

 NAME
   MMA_Video_Width (V1) [..G.Q], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Width of the decoded picture in pixels, as published by the demuxer, and 0
   until this class has taken its data in. A geometry of 0 or of 65536 and
   more is refused, since neither can be a picture this class can decode.

   The decoded picture has to come out at the published width. A picture
   which comes out at another size fails the conversion, and the pull which
   triggers the decode returns 0.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.decoder/MMA_Video_Height, avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_Width

avif.decoder/MMM_Pull

 NAME
   MMM_Pull -- Pulls the decoded picture in ARGB32. (V1)

 SYNOPSIS
   ULONG DoMethod(Object *obj, MMM_Pull, ULONG port, APTR buffer, ULONG
   length);

 FUNCTION
   Port 1 hands out the decoded picture: four bytes per pixel, alpha, red,
   green, blue, top row first, tightly packed and with no padding between
   rows. The read position is in the object, so a picture can be pulled in as
   many pieces as the consumer wants, and a short count is returned when the
   end of the picture is in the way. Once the last pixel has been handed out a
   pull returns 0 and reports MMERR_END_OF_DATA through MMA_ErrorCode.

   The length is rounded down to whole pixels. A length too short for a
   single pixel cannot be satisfied and is refused with
   MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS rather than writing four bytes into a buffer which
   cannot hold them.

   The first pull on port 1 is where the picture is decoded: it takes the
   data in if nothing has done so yet, runs dav1d over the colour stream and
   over the alpha stream if there is one, and converts the result into the
   whole bitmap before any of it is handed out. Later pulls only copy.

   A pull which cannot produce data returns 0 and says why through
   MMA_ErrorCode: an input which publishes no AvifInfo is
   MMERR_NO_STREAM, a refused geometry, a stream dav1d will not decode or a
   picture whose size does not match the published geometry is
   MMERR_WRONG_DATA, and a failed allocation is MMERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY. Each of
   them is written to the plugin log as well, so a short pull tells a consumer
   that the picture ends there and an error code tells it why.

   Port 0 is a passthrough to the input stream. This class does not use it:
   the picture is decoded out of the data avif.demuxer published, not out of
   what is pulled through the port.

   A NULL buffer, a length of 0 or a port other than 0 or 1 is refused with
   MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS.

 INPUTS
   obj - object to perform method on.
   port - number of port, 0 for the passthrough or 1 for the picture.
   buffer - destination buffer for the data.
   length - amount of data to be pulled in *bytes*.

 RESULT
   Number of bytes pulled, secondary error information via MMA_ErrorCode.

 SEE ALSO
   multimedia.class/MMA_ErrorCode, avif.demuxer/MMM_Pull


